    "result_text": "All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 4 tests, 0 failures). The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nAlongside the existing human-readable audit line, owner creation now emits an immutable structured `OWNER_CREATED` event to the `AUDIT` logger as JSON \u2014 e.g. `{\"seq\":1,\"ownerId\":11,\"customerCode\":\"NSW-16E6609E\",\"membershipLevel\":1,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}`.\n\nI kept each piece of logic in a small, single-responsibility unit:\n\n- **`Owner.primaryIdentifier()`** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 the single source of truth for \"whatever identifies this owner right now.\" It returns `customerCode` today; when the customer code is unified into a member id, only this one method changes and the event automatically carries the memberId. This directly satisfies the spec's requirement that the event carry the *current* primary identifier.\n\n- **`OwnerCreatedEvent`** (`rest/OwnerCreatedEvent.java`) \u2014 an immutable Java `record` holding the event data `{seq, ownerId, customerCode, membershipLevel, event}` in exactly that field order. Its convenience constructor sources the identifier from `owner.primaryIdentifier()` and the level from the existing `MembershipLevelCalculator.effectiveMembershipLevel(...)`, reusing established logic rather than duplicating it.\n\n- **`OwnerCreatedEventEmitter`** (`rest/OwnerCreatedEventEmitter.java`) \u2014 a `@Component` that owns the monotonic `AtomicLong` sequence (increasing across all creates), stamps each event, serializes it with the injected Jackson `ObjectMapper`, and logs it to `AUDIT`.\n\n- **`OwnerAuditLogger`** \u2014 its stated job is \"emit audit-trail entries for owner lifecycle events, keeping audit concerns out of the controller,\" so I had its existing `ownerCreated(owner)` delegate the structured event to the emitter. The controller is unchanged \u2014 it still makes one audit call \u2014 and no class became a catch-all.",
